1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Welcome back. A Claude.md file is where you hand Claude your standing rules,

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:10,000
and it loads at the start of every session. Get it right, and Claude works the way your team does.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:15,000
This lesson walks through what makes a rule effective, what it costs, and why you commit it.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Let's dig in. Before we write a single rule, let's separate three things people constantly mix up,

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:28,000
because they behave very differently. First, Claude.md itself. These are developer-authored

6
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:33,000
rules that you write, and they load into context at the start of every session and apply to every

7
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:41,000
turn. Second, agent memory. That is durable state the agent itself writes and updates as it works,

8
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:48,000
and it survives context compaction and even session boundaries. Third, the context window.

9
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:54,000
That is the ephemeral working space for the current session, and it truncates as things fill up,

10
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:01,000
so nothing living there persists on its own. Claude.md is the one you author by hand,

11
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:07,000
and that is what this lesson is about. Here is the most important mindset to start with.

12
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Claude treats your Claude.md as context to consider, not as configuration it is forced to

13
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:20,000
obey. The rules are guidance the model reads and weighs, not a hard constraint wired into the

14
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:26,000
system. That means a rule can be missed, especially when the context gets crowded and your

15
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:31,000
instruction is buried under everything else. So if an action genuinely has to be blocked,

16
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:37,000
a destructive command for example, don't rely on a written rule to stop it. Reach for a pre-tool

17
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:44,000
use hook instead, which runs deterministically before the tool executes and can deny the action

18
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:49,000
outright. Write rules to steer behavior and use hooks when you need real enforcement.

19
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:58,000
So what actually earns a place in your Claude.md? Three kinds of content pull their weight. First,

20
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:03,000
project structure. Tell Claude where things live, the key directories, and how the code base is

21
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:10,000
organized so it navigates without hunting around. Second, conventions. Spell out your coding style,

22
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:16,000
your naming, and the patterns the team follows. Stated plainly so the output matches your norms

23
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:23,000
instead of generic defaults. Third, commands. Put the exact build, test, and lint commands

24
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:29,000
right in the file, written verbatim, so Claude can copy and run them. Notice the theme. Every

25
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:34,000
one of these saves Claude from guessing something your team already knows, and that is what makes a

26
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:41,000
rule worth its tokens. Now the cost side, because this trips people up. Every line you put in

27
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:48,000
Claude.md loads into the context window, so treat that space as a budget you are spending. And here

28
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:54,000
is the key detail. The whole file loads in full, regardless of how long it is. There is no

29
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:00,000
summarizing and no truncation to fit. So a bloated file crowds out room the model needs for the

30
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:05,000
actual work in front of it. A good target is to keep it under a couple hundred lines.

31
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:11,000
The point to internalize is simple. Claude.md isn't free memory sitting off to the side.

32
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:16,000
It is tokens you pay for on every session, so spend them only on rules that earn their place.

33
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:22,000
Let's contrast two ways people write these files, because the difference decides whether the rules

34
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:30,000
actually work. On one side, scoped rules. They are short, specific, and testable. Structure,

35
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:36,000
conventions, and commands, each stated as a clear directive, the model can act on every turn

36
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:42,000
without guessing what you meant. On the other side, verbose prose. Long paragraphs of background

37
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:50,000
and rationale that read like documentation. The trouble is they burn context. They bury the one

38
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:56,000
instruction that mattered, and they make it harder for Claude to find and follow it. Same intent,

39
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:02,000
very different results. When you catch yourself explaining why at length, stop, and rewrite it

40
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:10,000
as a short directive that says what to do. Here is where Claude.md pays off for a whole team,

41
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:16,000
not just you. A project Claude.md lives at the repository root and travels through source

42
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:22,000
control like any other file, so when you commit it, every teammate who clones the repo inherits

43
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:27,000
the exact same conventions automatically. A new contributor gets your build commands on day one

44
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:33,000
without asking anyone or reading a stale wiki, and behavior stays consistent because everyone's

45
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:39,000
sessions start from the same shared brief. Compare that to the alternative, where the real build

46
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:45,000
command lives in one senior engineer's head. Shared rules in version control beat tribal

47
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:50,000
knowledge every time, and they update the moment someone improves the file and pushes it.

48
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:58,000
Let's get precise about how Claude.md actually reaches the model, because three details matter.

49
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:04,000
First, it is loaded at the start. The file is injected at the beginning of every conversation,

50
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:10,000
so its rules are already present before you type your first request. Second, and this surprises

51
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:17,000
people, it arrives as a user or project message. It rides alongside the system prompt rather than

52
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:24,000
being baked into the system prompt itself. Third, it is loaded in full. Unlike capped memory files

53
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:31,000
that only read their first stretch, the entire Claude.md loads no matter how long it is. Those

54
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:38,000
three facts explain both its power and its cost. It is always there, it steers from the conversation,

55
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:44,000
and it always spends its full weight in tokens. Let's turn all of that into how you actually write

56
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:51,000
a good rule. The best rules read like directives, not like documentation, so make each one easy to

57
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:57,000
act on. Use imperative, scoped statements, do this, avoid that, rather than paragraphs of background.

58
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:04,000
Put commands in verbatim so they are copy-ready and there is no ambiguity about the exact flag

59
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:09,000
or path, and trim anything that doesn't change behavior, because every line you keep competes

60
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:16,000
for the model's attention. The habit to build is ruthless editing, short and specific wins,

61
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:22,000
because a crowded file dilutes every rule inside it. If a line wouldn't change what Claude does,

62
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:30,000
it probably doesn't belong. Claude.md is your standing brief to Claude, loaded every session.

63
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:35,000
It holds structure, conventions, and commands, and it counts against your context window.

64
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:40,000
Just remember, it's guidance to consider, not enforcement, so reach for a hook when you need

65
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:46,000
to block something. That covers writing effective Claude.md rules. I'll see you in the next lesson.

